---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---
<Base
  title="What ImageIO gives you free — LightPhotos Blog"
  desc="One call to Apple's ImageIO was doing far more for LightPhotos than I realised. Porting away from it put a number on exactly how much."
  active="blogs"
>
  <main class="lp-page">
    <article class="lp-article">
      <header class="lp-article-head">
        <p class="lp-article-meta"><a href="/blogs.html">Blogs</a> &middot; <time datetime="2026-07-20">July 20, 2026</time></p>
        <h1>What ImageIO gives you free, and what it costs to leave</h1>
        <p class="lp-article-dek">
          One call to Apple's ImageIO was doing far more for LightPhotos than I
          realised. Porting to Linux, Windows and the browser put a number on
          exactly how much.
        </p>
        <p class="lp-article-byline">Andrew Yao &middot; 4 min read</p>
      </header>

      <p>
        LightPhotos started out Mac-only. When I ported it, I expected the
        painful parts to be the window, the graphics and the file dialogs. They
        were not. The painful part was the thing I had stopped thinking about
        years ago, which is reading an image file.
      </p>

      <h2>The one call</h2>
      <p>
        ImageIO has a function that means "give me this image, already scaled
        down to about this size". Not "decode this, and then I will resize it".
        The scaling is part of the request.
      </p>
      <p>
        For a JPEG that is a convenience. For a camera RAW file it is everything.
        A RAW file is not a picture. It is unprocessed sensor readout, and
        reconstructing full colour from it is expensive.
      </p>
      <p>
        Almost every RAW file also carries a small JPEG, written by the camera
        so its own screen had something to display. Ask ImageIO for a small
        version and it goes and finds that preview, skipping the sensor maths
        entirely. On Sony ARW files I measured 11 ms instead of 170 to 210.
      </p>
      <p>None of that was code I wrote. It was one function argument.</p>

      <h2>Nothing else has that function</h2>

      <figure class="lp-figure">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 234" role="img" aria-label="Comparison: on macOS a single ImageIO call handles scaled decoding; everywhere else the program walks EXIF tags to find an embedded preview and falls back to a full sensor decode.">
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="12">MACOS</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-panel" x="0" y="22" width="290" height="192" rx="10"></rect>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="26" y="90" width="238" height="56" rx="8"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-t" x="145" y="113" text-anchor="middle">One call</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="145" y="133" text-anchor="middle">scaling is part of the request</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="145" y="180" text-anchor="middle">11 ms to a RAW preview</text>

          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="350" y="12">LINUX, WINDOWS, BROWSER</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-panel" x="350" y="22" width="290" height="192" rx="10"></rect>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="430" y="40" width="130" height="28" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="495" y="59" text-anchor="middle">RAW file</text>
          <line class="lp-fig-line" x1="495" y1="68" x2="495" y2="76"></line>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M489 76 L501 76 L495 84 Z"></path>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="375" y="88" width="240" height="28" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="495" y="107" text-anchor="middle">Walk the TIFF and EXIF tags</text>
          <path class="lp-fig-line" d="M495 116 V128 H424 V136 M495 128 H566 V136"></path>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M418 136 L430 136 L424 144 Z M560 136 L572 136 L566 144 Z"></path>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="360" y="144" width="128" height="46" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="424" y="164" text-anchor="middle">Decode the</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="424" y="179" text-anchor="middle">embedded JPEG</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="502" y="144" width="128" height="46" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="566" y="164" text-anchor="middle">rawler:</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="566" y="179" text-anchor="middle">demosaic the sensor</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="424" y="206" text-anchor="middle">fast path</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="566" y="206" text-anchor="middle">fallback</text>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>
          There is no drop-in equivalent on any other platform. I checked before
          committing to the port, and everywhere I looked the answer was that
          you build it yourself.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Building it yourself means knowing that RAW files are mostly TIFF
        containers, that TIFF containers have a tag structure, and that the
        embedded preview sits at a known place inside it. I now know all of
        that, and I would rather not.
      </p>

      <div class="lp-compare-wrap">
        <table class="lp-compare">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col"></th>
              <th scope="col">macOS, ImageIO</th>
              <th scope="col">Linux, Windows, browser</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Embedded RAW preview</th>
              <td>11 ms, built in</td>
              <td>Hand-rolled from EXIF tags</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Sensor decode to screen size</th>
              <td>170 to 210 ms</td>
              <td>680 ms, after optimising</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">HEIC</th>
              <td>Yes</td>
              <td>No pure-Rust decoder exists</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Linear DNG</th>
              <td>Cannot decode it</td>
              <td>Byte-exact against ground truth</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2>Two steps in the wrong order</h2>
      <p>
        That 680 ms started life as 1.80 seconds. My code was running noise
        reduction at full sensor resolution and only shrinking the image
        afterwards.
      </p>

      <figure class="lp-figure">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 176" role="img" aria-label="Pipeline comparison: denoising at 24 megapixels then shrinking takes 1.80 seconds; shrinking first then denoising takes 0.68 seconds.">
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="12">A 2560 PX PREVIEW FROM A 24 MP SONY ARW</text>

          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="0" y="26" width="142" height="32" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="71" y="46" text-anchor="middle">Decode sensor</text>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M146 36 L146 48 L156 42 Z"></path>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box lp-fig-bad-stroke" x="160" y="26" width="142" height="32" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="231" y="46" text-anchor="middle">Denoise at 24 MP</text>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M306 36 L306 48 L316 42 Z"></path>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="320" y="26" width="142" height="32" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="391" y="46" text-anchor="middle">Shrink to 2560</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar-dim" x="0" y="66" width="462" height="7" rx="3.5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="476" y="52">1.80 s &middot; peak 1.39 GB</text>

          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="0" y="106" width="142" height="32" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="71" y="126" text-anchor="middle">Decode sensor</text>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M146 116 L146 128 L156 122 Z"></path>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="160" y="106" width="142" height="32" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="231" y="126" text-anchor="middle">Shrink to 2560</text>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M306 116 L306 128 L316 122 Z"></path>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="320" y="106" width="142" height="32" rx="6"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="391" y="126" text-anchor="middle">Denoise at 2560</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar" x="0" y="146" width="175" height="7" rx="3.5"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="476" y="132">0.68 s &middot; peak 1.21 GB</text>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>
          Two lines swapped. The output is byte-identical at full size.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        Swapping the two steps around was a two-line change and it saved 1.1
        seconds per photo.
      </p>

      <h2>The other costs</h2>
      <p>
        Formats were the second one. ImageIO reads HEIC, the format iPhones use,
        and no pure-Rust HEIC decoder exists. My only option is binding to a C
        library, which drags a build toolchain onto every platform I was trying
        to keep easy to build. So HEIC is still Mac-only, and that is a
        difference users can see.
      </p>
      <p>
        The third was a dependency that would not run in a browser. The RAW
        library calls the standard clock to log how long a decode took, and
        under WebAssembly there is nothing behind that function, so calling it
        aborts. My build now fetches the library, applies a four-line patch and
        compiles from that copy. Cargo cannot patch one target only, so every
        build depends on it.
      </p>

      <h2>Apple's decoder is not always right</h2>
      <p>
        Part of my porting plan was a fidelity check. Decode a test file both
        ways, confirm they agree, treat Apple's output as the reference.
      </p>
      <p>
        Apple's decoder could not open the file. Not a wrong answer, not a
        close-enough answer. It cannot decode Linear DNG at all.
      </p>
      <p>
        So I rebuilt the test around ground truth I worked out myself. I
        generated a synthetic DNG holding a known mathematical gradient and
        computed every pixel on paper. The Rust decoder matched exactly, with
        zero mismatches across 147,456 samples.
      </p>
      <blockquote class="lp-pullquote">
        Two decoders agreeing only tells you they agree. Matching arithmetic you
        did yourself tells you one of them is right.
      </blockquote>

      <h2>Would I use it again?</h2>
      <p>
        Yes, and I still do on the Mac. What you get is real. Decades of format
        support, a fast path you would never have thought to build, and
        correctness on file formats that are largely reverse-engineered and full
        of vendor quirks.
      </p>
      <p>
        What you pay is portability, plus one category of bug you cannot fix.
        When Apple's decoder cannot open a Linear DNG there is no patch and no
        workaround short of shipping a second decoder. You are not a user of
        that code, you are downstream of it.
      </p>

      <h2>What I learned</h2>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Find out what the framework does for you before you need to
          leave.</strong> Not by reading the docs, but by writing the
          replacement and measuring both.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A platform dependency is a purchase, so name the
          price.</strong> Speed, formats, portability, and one class of bug you
          cannot fix. I priced three of those four wrong.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Have a fallback for the reference being wrong.</strong> The
          gradient I could compute on paper turned out to be the stronger test.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Check the order of your pipeline steps.</strong> Shrinking
          before denoising rather than after was worth 1.1 seconds per photo,
          and it was two lines.
        </li>
      </ul>




